argument ninth-room "The Ninth Room"
meta profile="cludia", version="0.1.0", cludia-next-ids="v1;P=211;L=1;C=1;CP=7;J=74"

premise[fact] P1:case-determine-who-killed-ruth-dallow-aldous-bell ::T "[CASE] Determine who killed Ruth Dallow and Aldous Bell, and explain the false object, false ledger account, and locked study."

premise[fact] P2:observed-18-october-1924-aldous-bell-announced-9 ::T "[OBSERVED] On 18 October 1924, Aldous Bell announced at 9:00 p.m. that at 10:30 he would identify a false collection object, a false ledger account, and Ruth Dallow's killer."

premise[fact] P3:observed-aldous-carried-unopened-brown-wrapped-parcel-bearing ::T "[OBSERVED] Aldous carried an unopened brown-wrapped parcel bearing a faded bookseller's label into the Ninth Room study at 9:05 p.m."

premise[fact] P4:testimony-mrs-wren-mrs-wren-watched-aldous-enter ::T "[TESTIMONY—Mrs. Wren] Mrs. Wren watched Aldous enter the study and lock its only door from inside after he instructed that no one enter until he rang twice."

premise[fact] P5:testimony-mrs-wren-9-18-handbell-rang-once ::T "[TESTIMONY—Mrs. Wren] At 9:18 the handbell rang once; before she knocked, a voice she identified as Aldous told her to leave the hot water and await two rings."

premise[fact] P6:observed-hot-water-tray-remained-untouched-corridor-table ::T "[OBSERVED] The hot-water tray remained untouched on the corridor table."

premise[fact] P7:observed-10-30-study-door-was-locked-its ::T "[OBSERVED] At 10:30 the study door was locked, its only key visible on the inside; the windows were latched and iron bars crossed the chimney."

premise[fact] P8:observed-felix-harrow-cassian-vale-broke-down-study ::T "[OBSERVED] Felix Harrow and Cassian Vale broke down the study door."

premise[fact] P9:observed-aldous-was-dead-his-desk-kneeling-carpet ::T "[OBSERVED] Aldous was dead at his desk, kneeling on the carpet with his torso across the blotter and his right hand clutching his coat above the heart."

premise[fact] P10:observed-tiny-puncture-faint-blue-black-stain-marked ::T "[OBSERVED] A tiny puncture with a faint blue-black stain marked the fleshy base of Aldous's left thumb; no obvious weapon was present."

premise[fact] P11:observed-parcel-was-open-contained-gray-vellum-book ::T "[OBSERVED] The parcel was open and contained a gray vellum book titled A Dissertation Concerning the Tides, purportedly printed in 1681; its brass clasp was open."

premise[fact] P12:observed-only-book-first-sixteen-leaves-had-been ::T "[OBSERVED] Only the book's first sixteen leaves had been cut open along their folded edges; the remaining text block was unopened."

premise[fact] P13:observed-aldous-spectacles-were-folded-inside-their-case ::T "[OBSERVED] Aldous's spectacles were folded inside their case."

premise[fact] P14:observed-desk-clock-had-stopped-9-27-although ::T "[OBSERVED] The desk clock had stopped at 9:27 although its glass was intact."

premise[fact] P15:observed-chair-stood-nearly-three-feet-behind-its ::T "[OBSERVED] The chair stood nearly three feet behind its usual position, with a fresh scrape running backward from the desk to its front legs."

premise[fact] P16:observed-desk-was-perpendicular-cold-fireplace-its-left ::T "[OBSERVED] The desk was perpendicular to the cold fireplace, and its left corner was less than two feet from the fender."

premise[fact] P17:document-observed-burned-fragment-ruled-paper-against-fireplace ::T "[DOCUMENT/OBSERVED] A burned fragment of ruled paper against the fireplace fender retained the text 'ROW—17'."

premise[fact] P18:document-observed-aldous-left-unfinished-sentence-mistake-was ::T "[DOCUMENT/OBSERVED] Aldous left an unfinished sentence: 'The mistake was assuming that Ruth had discovered—'."

premise[fact] P19:testimony-group-no-one-admitted-sending-parcel ::T "[TESTIMONY—group] No one admitted sending the parcel."

premise[fact] P20:background-ruth-dallow-assistant-cataloguer-beatrice-orr-died ::T "[BACKGROUND] Ruth Dallow, assistant cataloguer to Beatrice Orr, died three nights earlier beneath a freight wagon at Fallowmere Station; the preliminary finding was accidental death."

premise[fact] P21:background-fallowmere-station-had-passenger-platforms-parcels-office ::T "[BACKGROUND] Fallowmere Station had passenger platforms, a parcels office, telegraph counter, and freight yard; Greyhaven was one stop inland."

premise[fact] P22:constraint-no-secret-passage-disguised-body-supernatural-event ::T "[CONSTRAINT] No secret passage, disguised body, supernatural event, or unintroduced culprit is permitted."

lemma[fact] P23:inference-provisional-puncture-blue-black-stain-make-book "[INFERENCE—provisional] The puncture and blue-black stain make the book clasp or another small contact device a plausible delivery mechanism for poison or injury."
  <- AND#J1(P10, P11)

lemma[fact] P24:inference-provisional-unopened-bulk-book-cased-spectacles-suggest "[INFERENCE—provisional] The unopened bulk of the book and cased spectacles suggest Aldous may not have been reading normally; his interaction may have been limited to opening or inspecting the clasp and first leaves."
  <- AND#J2(P11, P12, P13)

lemma[fact] P25:inference-provisional-displaced-chair-scrape-may-record-sudden "[INFERENCE—provisional] The displaced chair and scrape may record a sudden backward reaction or deliberate manipulation and could help reconstruct the death."
  <- AND#J3(P15, P16)

lemma[fact] P26:inference-provisional-stopped-clock-may-indicate-event-9 "[INFERENCE—provisional] The stopped clock may indicate an event at 9:27, but without a demonstrated cause it is not yet a reliable time of death."
  <- AND#J4(P14, P15)

lemma[fact] P27:inference-provisional-9-18-voice-establishes-aldous-was "[INFERENCE—provisional] The 9:18 voice establishes Aldous was likely alive then only if Mrs. Wren's identification was accurate and the voice was contemporaneous rather than imitated or mechanically reproduced."
  <- AND#J5(P5, P36)

premise[fact] P28:question-what-does-row-17-identify-ledger-location ::T "[QUESTION] What does 'ROW—17' identify: a ledger location, collection shelf/catalogue position, railway/freight reference, or something else?"

premise[fact] P29:question-who-prepared-delivered-parcel-when-did-it ::T "[QUESTION] Who prepared and delivered the parcel, and when did it enter Vesper House?"

premise[fact] P30:question-what-did-ruth-actually-discover-given-aldous ::T "[QUESTION] What did Ruth actually discover, given Aldous's warning that the prevailing assumption about her discovery was mistaken?"

premise[fact] P31:question-which-person-had-motive-access-technical-means ::T "[QUESTION] Which person had motive, access, and technical means to connect the false object, false account, Ruth's death, and Aldous's locked-room death?"

premise[fact] P32:background-miriam-bell-aldous-niece-was-pharmaceutical-chemist ::T "[BACKGROUND] Miriam Bell, Aldous's niece, was a pharmaceutical chemist."

premise[fact] P33:background-felix-harrow-was-aldous-private-secretary ::T "[BACKGROUND] Felix Harrow was Aldous's private secretary."

premise[fact] P34:background-dr-lenora-pike-was-aldous-physician-oldest ::T "[BACKGROUND] Dr. Lenora Pike was Aldous's physician and oldest friend."

premise[fact] P35:background-gideon-rusk-was-solicitor-bell-estate ::T "[BACKGROUND] Gideon Rusk was solicitor to the Bell estate."

premise[fact] P36:background-cassian-vale-was-illusionist-whose-apparatus-aldous ::T "[BACKGROUND] Cassian Vale was an illusionist whose apparatus Aldous collected."

premise[fact] P37:background-beatrice-orr-was-curator-vesper-collection-ruth ::T "[BACKGROUND] Beatrice Orr was curator of the Vesper collection and Ruth Dallow's supervisor."

premise[fact] P38:background-constable-merritt-was-present-return-ruth-effects ::T "[BACKGROUND] Constable Merritt was present to return Ruth's effects and ask Aldous final questions; he was not one of the six dinner guests and was stranded by the tide."

premise[fact] P39:constraint-method-neutral-narration-reliable-character-attributed-claims ::T "[CONSTRAINT/METHOD] Neutral narration is reliable, character-attributed claims are testimony unless corroborated, and documents may deceive as to author or content."

premise[fact] P40:testimony-dr-pike-aldous-thumb-puncture-was-recent ::T "[TESTIMONY—Dr. Pike] Aldous's thumb puncture was recent; the blue-black color was foreign marking dye rather than bruising."

premise[fact] P41:testimony-dr-pike-aldous-posture-pupils-were-consistent ::T "[TESTIMONY—Dr. Pike] Aldous's posture and pupils were consistent with aconitine poisoning, but chemical analysis would be required to prove it."

premise[fact] P42:testimony-dr-pike-aconitine-introduced-puncture-could-permit ::T "[TESTIMONY—Dr. Pike] Aconitine introduced by puncture could permit speech for several minutes and kill through cardiac disruption ten to thirty minutes later."

premise[fact] P43:testimony-dr-pike-aldous-had-longstanding-irregular-heartbeat ::T "[TESTIMONY—Dr. Pike] Aldous had a longstanding irregular heartbeat; an attack could mimic some poisoning signs but would not explain the puncture or dye."

premise[fact] P44:testimony-mrs-wren-about-6-12-p-m ::T "[TESTIMONY—Mrs. Wren] At about 6:12 p.m. the study clock showed exactly 6:00, and Aldous instructed her not to correct it."

premise[fact] P45:testimony-beatrice-collector-evaluating-genuinely-unopened-early-book ::T "[TESTIMONY—Beatrice] A collector evaluating a genuinely unopened early book would inspect paper, type, sewing, and binding before cutting additional folded leaves."

premise[fact] P46:observed-first-sixteen-leaves-were-two-old-octavo ::T "[OBSERVED] The first sixteen leaves were two old octavo gatherings, while the later block was newer and the spine unusually thick."

premise[fact] P47:testimony-felix-parcel-weight-surprised-aldous ::T "[TESTIMONY—Felix] The parcel's weight surprised Aldous."

premise[fact] P48:observed-clasp-inner-face-had-precisely-machined-blue ::T "[OBSERVED] The clasp's inner face had a precisely machined, blue-black-stained narrow groove that was empty but had held something slender."

premise[fact] P49:observed-when-pressed-shut-spoon-handle-clasp-resisted ::T "[OBSERVED] When pressed shut with a spoon handle, the clasp resisted and then snapped closed with disproportionate force."

premise[fact] P50:testimony-cassian-cassian-identified-spring-catch-when-asked ::T "[TESTIMONY—Cassian] Cassian identified a spring catch and, when asked whether he had seen another fitting behave similarly, answered, 'Not this fitting,' then refused to elaborate."

premise[fact] P51:observed-fallowmere-carrier-delivered-parcel-4-00-p ::T "[OBSERVED] A Fallowmere carrier delivered the parcel at 4:00 p.m.; its wrapper bore an obsolete printed March & Rowe Antiquarian Booksellers label."

premise[fact] P52:testimony-gideon-march-rowe-had-ceased-trading-five ::T "[TESTIMONY—Gideon] March & Rowe had ceased trading five years earlier."

premise[fact] P53:testimony-beatrice-aldous-had-bought-forty-six-old ::T "[TESTIMONY—Beatrice] Aldous had bought forty-six old March & Rowe labels, stored with packing materials in the Bell estate office; Felix and Gideon regularly used the office, while Beatrice sometimes withdrew labels for museum shipments."

premise[fact] P54:testimony-felix-felix-accepted-parcel-vesper-house ::T "[TESTIMONY—Felix] Felix accepted the parcel at Vesper House."

premise[fact] P55:observed-parcel-had-no-invoice-sender-address-covering ::T "[OBSERVED] The parcel had no invoice, sender's address, or covering letter."

premise[fact] P56:testimony-felix-quoting-aldous-before-opening-parcel-aldous ::T "[TESTIMONY—Felix quoting Aldous] Before opening the parcel, Aldous said its sender did not expect him to read what was inside the book."

premise[fact] P57:observed-document-beneath-obsolete-label-wrapper-was-typed ::T "[OBSERVED/DOCUMENT] Beneath the obsolete label, the wrapper was typed 'ONE VELLUM VOLUME — TIDAL DISSERTATION'; the capital M in VOLUME sat slightly high."

premise[fact] P58:observed-felix-brought-three-estate-office-registers-vesper ::T "[OBSERVED] Felix brought three estate-office registers to Vesper House that afternoon for Aldous's quarterly review."

premise[fact] P59:document-packing-material-register-says-march-rowe-labels ::T "[DOCUMENT] The packing-material register says 'MARCH & ROWE LABELS — BUNDLE OF 46'; routine users F.H. and G.R.; museum withdrawals B.O."

premise[fact] P60:document-aldous-attribution-unverified-memorandum-aldous-hand-says ::T "[DOCUMENT—Aldous attribution unverified] A memorandum in Aldous's hand says 'JUNE SECURITY REVIEW — G.R. TO ASSIST' and lists signature stamps, duplicate/spare keys, chemical stores, locks, alarm fittings, safes, and strong-room miscellany."

premise[fact] P61:document-chemical-stock-index-says-30-september-cabinet ::T "[DOCUMENT] The chemical-stock index says '30 SEPTEMBER — CABINET INVENTORY VERIFIED' with initials M.B."

lemma[fact] P62:inference-provisional-machined-spring-clasp-leading-candidate-delivering "[INFERENCE—provisional] The machined spring clasp is the leading candidate for delivering Aldous's dyed thumb puncture."
  <- AND#J6(P40, P48, P49)

lemma[fact] P63:inference-provisional-missing-slender-item-clasp-groove-may "[INFERENCE—provisional] The missing slender item from the clasp groove may have been a needle or projectile, but its disappearance and exact action remain unexplained."
  <- AND#J7(P48, P62)

lemma[fact] P64:inference-provisional-constructing-deploying-parcel-trap-likely-required "[INFERENCE—provisional] Constructing and deploying the parcel trap likely required some combination of mechanical knowledge, access to aconitine or chemical stores, access to an old label, and a way to deliver the parcel."
  <- AND#J8(P48, P53, P61)

lemma[fact] P65:inference-provisional-access-obsolete-march-rowe-labels-implicates "[INFERENCE—provisional] Access to the obsolete March & Rowe labels implicates opportunity for Felix, Gideon, and Beatrice but does not identify which person used one."
  <- AND#J9(P53, P59)

premise[fact] P66:inference-provisional-raised-capital-m-potential-typewriter-fingerprint ::T "[INFERENCE—provisional] The raised capital M is a potential typewriter fingerprint that can identify the wrapper's typist if matched to another sample."

lemma[fact] P67:inference-provisional-burned-row-17-fragment-may-refer "[INFERENCE—provisional] The burned 'ROW—17' fragment may refer to a March & Rowe ledger/account entry, making it a stronger candidate link to Aldous's announced false account."
  <- AND#J10(P17, P51, P59)

lemma[fact] P69:inference-provisional-aconitine-would-exploit-aldous-cardiac-history "[INFERENCE—provisional] Aconitine would exploit Aldous's cardiac history by making murder resemble a natural fatal attack."
  <- AND#J12(P41, P43)

lemma[fact] P70:inference-provisional-volume-composite-object-old-front-newer "[INFERENCE—provisional] The volume is a composite object with an old front and newer, thick rear block, making it the leading candidate for Aldous's announced false collection object."
  <- AND#J13(P11, P46)

lemma[fact] P71:inference-provisional-thick-newer-block-surprising-weight-suggest "[INFERENCE—provisional] The thick newer block and surprising weight suggest the book may conceal apparatus or another object rather than merely substituted paper."
  <- AND#J14(P46, P47)

lemma[fact] P72:assessment-part-ii-distributes-relevant-capabilities-among-several "[ASSESSMENT] Part II distributes relevant capabilities among several people and does not yet provide a uniquely identifying combination."
  <- AND#J15(P32, P33, P34, P35, P36, P37, P53, P60, P61)

lemma[fact] P73:inference-provisional-cassian-phrase-not-this-fitting-suggests "[INFERENCE—provisional] Cassian's phrase 'Not this fitting' suggests he recognized the spring mechanism from a different object or installation, not that the principle was unfamiliar to him."
  <- AND#J16(P49, P50)

lemma[fact] P74:suspect-ranking-provisional-gideon-currently-has-broadest-documented "[SUSPECT RANKING—provisional] Gideon currently has the broadest documented crossover opportunity: estate/account involvement, routine label access, and participation in a review covering chemical stores and mechanical security fittings; the documents may nevertheless be misleading or planted."
  <- AND#J17(P35, P53, P60)

lemma[fact] P75:inference-provisional-if-clock-was-running-twelve-minutes "[INFERENCE—provisional] If the clock was running twelve minutes slow at 6:12, its stopped display of 9:27 corresponds to about 9:39 actual time, twenty-one minutes after the 9:18 voice and within Pike's stated aconitine death window."
  <- AND#J18(P14, P42, P44)

premise[fact] P76:testimony-felix-felix-entered-billiard-room-9-25 ::T "[TESTIMONY—Felix] Felix entered the billiard room at 9:25 to deliver Gideon a telegram and saw Gideon and Cassian there."

premise[fact] P77:testimony-merritt-9-25-9-50-merritt-continuously ::T "[TESTIMONY—Merritt] From 9:25 to 9:50 Merritt continuously watched the billiard room's single propped-open door from the opposite hall; neither Gideon nor Cassian left."

premise[fact] P78:testimony-mrs-wren-mrs-wren-saw-gideon-cassian ::T "[TESTIMONY—Mrs. Wren] Mrs. Wren saw Gideon and Cassian in the billiard room shortly before 10:00."

premise[fact] P79:testimony-miriam-pike-miriam-claimed-be-alone-her ::T "[TESTIMONY—Miriam/Pike] Miriam claimed to be alone in her laboratory from shortly after 9:00 until Pike joined her around 9:40; Pike corroborated only that they remained together from approximately 9:40 until shortly after 10:00."

premise[fact] P80:observed-testimony-limit-neither-miriam-nor-pike-supplied ::T "[OBSERVED/TESTIMONY LIMIT] Neither Miriam nor Pike supplied the other with an alibi before approximately 9:40."

premise[fact] P81:testimony-felix-beatrice-felix-claimed-he-was-typing ::T "[TESTIMONY—Felix/Beatrice] Felix claimed he was typing in the library except for delivering the telegram; Beatrice claimed she was checking shelf records in the catalogue room; neither had a continuous witness."

premise[fact] P82:testimony-mrs-wren-room-directly-below-study-approximately ::T "[TESTIMONY—Mrs. Wren] From the room directly below the study at approximately 9:40, Wren heard a short overhead scrape like a chair abruptly driven across the floor and heard no cry."

premise[fact] P83:observed-document-sealed-aconitine-ampoule-was-missing-locked ::T "[OBSERVED/DOCUMENT] A sealed aconitine ampoule was missing from the locked poison cabinet; Miriam's September 30 inventory listed it as present."

premise[fact] P84:observed-miriam-current-poison-cabinet-key-remained-her ::T "[OBSERVED] Miriam's current poison-cabinet key remained on her watch chain, and the cabinet lock showed no damage."

premise[fact] P85:testimony-miriam-duplicate-poison-cabinet-key-had-been ::T "[TESTIMONY—Miriam] A duplicate poison-cabinet key had been borrowed by Aldous during his June inventory with Gideon; Aldous later said he had lost it."

premise[fact] P86:observed-document-aldous-broad-june-security-memorandum-mentions ::T "[OBSERVED/DOCUMENT] Aldous's broad June security memorandum mentions Gideon's review of spare keys and chemical stores but records nothing about the fate of a particular poison-cabinet key."

premise[fact] P87:testimony-cassian-cassian-designed-self-concealing-thorn-illusion ::T "[TESTIMONY—Cassian] Cassian designed the Self-Concealing Thorn illusion: a spring projected a fine needle and immediately withdrew it into an apparently solid fitting."

premise[fact] P88:testimony-cassian-cassian-said-his-only-personally-built ::T "[TESTIMONY—Cassian] Cassian said his only personally built Thorn mechanism was dismantled after injuring a stagehand."

premise[fact] P89:observed-background-thorn-plans-survived-1918-patent-dispute ::T "[OBSERVED/BACKGROUND] Thorn plans survived a 1918 patent dispute: Cassian retained originals, Aldous received a reference copy, Gideon kept a working court copy, and Felix handled Aldous's copy while cataloguing litigation papers."

premise[fact] P90:observed-it-was-not-yet-established-whether-book ::T "[OBSERVED] It was not yet established whether the book clasp copied the Thorn plans or only resembled them."

premise[fact] P91:document-9-25-telegram-read-retained-copy-dallow ::T "[DOCUMENT] The 9:25 telegram read: 'RETAINED COPY OF DALLOW MESSAGE LOCATED. SIGNATURE TYPED. WILL BRING AFTER TIDE.—FALLOWMERE STATIONMASTER.'"

premise[fact] P92:testimony-gideon-gideon-said-stationmaster-contacted-him-because ::T "[TESTIMONY—Gideon] Gideon said the stationmaster contacted him because he represented the Bell estate."

premise[fact] P93:testimony-gideon-gideon-characterized-retained-item-copy-message ::T "[TESTIMONY—Gideon] Gideon characterized the retained item as a copy of a message addressed to Ruth and said the stationmaster must explain why it was retained."

premise[fact] P94:observed-gideon-folded-stationmaster-telegram-along-its-original ::T "[OBSERVED] Gideon folded the stationmaster's telegram along its original crease."

lemma[fact] P95:inference-strong-approximately-9-40-overhead-scrape-corroborates "[INFERENCE—strong] The approximately 9:40 overhead scrape corroborates the displaced chair as the sound of Aldous's collapse and places his collapse near 9:40."
  <- AND#J19(P15, P82)

lemma[fact] P96:inference-strong-clock-running-roughly-twelve-minutes-slow "[INFERENCE—strong] A clock running roughly twelve minutes slow would show approximately 9:27–9:28 at a 9:39–9:40 collapse, so the clock and scrape independently converge on the same event time."
  <- AND#J20(P14, P44, P82, P95)

lemma[fact] P97:inference-gideon-cassian-9-25-9-50-alibi "[INFERENCE] Gideon and Cassian's 9:25–9:50 alibi does not exclude either from preparing a parcel trap earlier, because the clasp could kill without the maker entering the locked study."
  <- AND#J21(P7, P49, P77)

lemma[fact] P98:inference-conditional-if-september-30-inventory-was-accurate "[INFERENCE—conditional] If the September 30 inventory was accurate, the aconitine ampoule disappeared between September 30 and the evening of October 18."
  <- AND#J22(P61, P83)

lemma[fact] P99:inference-provisional-undamaged-cabinet-plus-secured-current-key "[INFERENCE—provisional] An undamaged cabinet plus the secured current key makes the reportedly lost duplicate key a leading route by which the aconitine could have been removed."
  <- AND#J23(P83, P84, P85)

lemma[fact] P100:inference-strong-self-concealing-thorn-principle-explains-how "[INFERENCE—strong] The Self-Concealing Thorn principle explains how the clasp could puncture Aldous and leave its stained external groove empty by retracting the needle into the thick composite volume."
  <- AND#J24(P10, P46, P48, P49, P87)

lemma[fact] P101:inference-provisional-direct-access-thorn-plans-presently-established "[INFERENCE—provisional] Direct access to Thorn plans is presently established for Cassian, Aldous, Gideon, and Felix."
  <- AND#J25(P89, P90)

lemma[fact] P102:inference-provisional-retained-dallow-message-likely-evidence-about "[INFERENCE—provisional] The retained Dallow message is likely evidence about Ruth's death, and its typed signature raises the possibility that she was deceived by a forged message."
  <- AND#J26(P20, P91, P93)

lemma[fact] P103:inference-provisional-wrapper-distinctive-raised-capital-m-may "[INFERENCE—provisional] The wrapper's distinctive raised capital M may be comparable to the retained Dallow message when the stationmaster supplies it."
  <- AND#J27(P57, P91)

lemma[fact] P104:suspect-ranking-provisional-gideon-remains-leading-single-person "[SUSPECT RANKING—provisional] Gideon remains the leading single-person fit because he combines Thorn plans, label access, estate-account involvement, and possible knowledge of the duplicate poison key; Felix is the closest alternative through plan and label access, parcel handling, typing, and unwitnessed movements."
  <- AND#J28(P33, P35, P53, P54, P60, P81, P85, P89)

lemma[fact] P105:inference-provisional-gideon-decision-begin-witnessed-billiards-game "[INFERENCE—provisional] Gideon's decision to begin a witnessed billiards game around 9:10 could be innocent, but it would also be a useful prearranged alibi for someone expecting a delayed-action poison trap to operate upstairs."
  <- AND#J29(P76, P77, P97)

premise[fact] P106:observed-background-ruth-normally-travelled-greyhaven-fallowmere-october ::T "[OBSERVED/BACKGROUND] Ruth normally travelled from Greyhaven to Fallowmere; on October 15 her outward ticket was used but the return portion remained in her handbag after death."

premise[fact] P107:testimony-porter-ruth-entered-fallowmere-parcels-office-around ::T "[TESTIMONY—porter] Ruth entered the Fallowmere parcels office around 8:20 p.m. and showed a telegram delivered to her at Vesper House less than an hour earlier."

premise[fact] P108:document-ruth-telegram-said-miss-dallow-come-parcels ::T "[DOCUMENT] Ruth's telegram said: 'MISS DALLOW—COME TO THE PARCELS OFFICE AT 8:20. BRING YOUR CATALOGUE NOTES.—A.B.'"

premise[fact] P109:document-observed-station-receipt-recorded-delivery-r-dallow ::T "[DOCUMENT/OBSERVED] A station receipt recorded delivery to 'R. DALLOW — VESPER GATE' at 7:24; the receipt signature was handwritten, while 'A.B.' on the telegram was typed."

premise[fact] P110:testimony-aldous-reported-narration-aldous-denied-sending-ruth ::T "[TESTIMONY—Aldous reported by narration] Aldous denied sending Ruth's summons when Merritt questioned him on October 16."

premise[fact] P111:observed-stationmaster-retained-copy-matched-ruth-telegram-both ::T "[OBSERVED] The stationmaster's retained copy matched Ruth's telegram, and both showed a capital M slightly higher than surrounding letters."

premise[fact] P112:observed-bell-estate-office-typewriter-produced-same-raised ::T "[OBSERVED] The Bell estate-office typewriter produced the same raised capital M; Felix routinely used it for estate correspondence and Gideon for legal and financial work."

premise[fact] P113:document-estate-office-daybook-october-15-says-g ::T "[DOCUMENT] The estate-office daybook for October 15 says G.R. entered at 6:32 for private use, a telegram form was taken to the station at 6:57, and G.R. returned the office key at 7:06."

premise[fact] P114:observed-document-telegraph-counter-stamped-ruth-summons-6 ::T "[OBSERVED/DOCUMENT] The telegraph counter stamped Ruth's summons at 6:59."

